.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt GETPID 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm getpid ,
.Nm gettid ,
.Nm getppid ,
.Nm getpgid ,
.Nm getpgrp ,
.Nm setpgid ,
.Nm getsid ,
.Nm setsid
.Nd get or set process, thread, process group, and session IDs
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In unistd.h
.Ft pid_t
.Fn getpid "void"
.Ft pid_t
.Fn gettid "void"
.Ft pid_t
.Fn getppid "void"
.Ft pid_t
.Fn getpgid "pid_t pid"
.Ft pid_t
.Fn getpgrp "void"
.Ft int
.Fn setpgid "pid_t pid" "pid_t pgid"
.Ft pid_t
.Fn getsid "pid_t pid"
.Ft pid_t
.Fn setsid "void"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn getpid
returns the calling process's ID, which is the ID of the thread that began
it.
.Fn gettid
returns the calling thread's ID, the process ID for that first thread.
.Fn getppid
returns the process ID of its parent, which is the same for every thread.
.Fn getpgid
returns the process group ID of
.Fa pid ;
.Fn getsid
returns its session ID.
For either call, a
.Fa pid
of 0 selects the caller.
.Fn getpgrp
takes no arguments and returns the caller's process group ID.
.Pp
.Fn setpgid
sets the process group ID of
.Fa pid
to
.Fa pgid .
A
.Fa pid
of 0 selects the caller.
A
.Fa pgid
of 0 selects the target's own process ID, making it a group leader.
The target must be the caller's process or its child and must not have
ended or be a session leader; any thread's ID names its process.
Every thread of the target is moved.
Unless the target is forming its own group, the destination group must
already have a member in the target's session.
The group's original leader need not still be alive.
.Pp
The kernel does not check whether a child has called
.Xr execve 2
or whether the child's session differs from the caller's session.
The destination group is checked against the target's session.
.Pp
.Fn setsid
creates a session and a process group, both with the caller's process ID.
The caller's process, every thread of it, becomes the leader of both.
A caller that already leads a process group cannot create a session.
The console session and controlling terminal are described in
.Xr console 4 .
.Pp
The i386 system call numbers are 20 for
.Fn getpid ,
224 for
.Fn gettid ,
64 for
.Fn getppid ,
132 for
.Fn getpgid ,
65 for
.Fn getpgrp ,
57 for
.Fn setpgid ,
147 for
.Fn getsid ,
and 66 for
.Fn setsid .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The query functions return the requested ID.
.Fn setsid
returns the new session ID, which is the caller's process ID.
.Fn setpgid
returns 0 on success.
On failure,
.Fn getpgid ,
.Fn getsid ,
.Fn setpgid
and
.Fn setsid
return -1 and set
.Va errno ;
musl's
.Fn getpid ,
.Fn gettid ,
.Fn getppid
and
.Fn getpgrp
return the kernel's result as it is.
The kernel interface returns the negative error number; its
.Er ESRCH
for no current process is the handler's condition, not one a program
meets.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/musl/schedmusl.c
checks that
.Fn gettid
returns the process ID, in a forked child too.
.Pa tests/jobprobe.c
forks a child, checks that its process group differs from its process ID
and that two session queries agree, calls
.Fn setpgid "0" "0" ,
and checks that the child's process group ID equals its process ID.
A separate child calls
.Fn setsid
and checks that both its session ID and process group ID equal its process ID.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EINVAL]"
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fn setpgid
was given a negative
.Fa pgid .
.It Bq Er EPERM
The target of
.Fn setpgid
is a session leader, or the destination is neither its own process ID
nor an existing group in its session.
For
.Fn setsid ,
the caller already leads a process group.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
There is no current process, or the process requested by
.Fn getpgid ,
.Fn getsid ,
or
.Fn setpgid
does not exist.
For
.Fn setpgid ,
this error also means that the target is unused, is a zombie, or is
neither the caller nor its child.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr kill 2 ,
.Xr waitpid 2 ,
.Xr console 4
